'''Madivaru Airport''' {{Airport codes|LMV|}} is an airport on Madivaru, [[Lhaviyani Atoll]], [[Maldives]].<ref name="TradeArabia">{{cite web |title=Maldives opens its second airport in Lhaviyani |url=https://www.tradearabia.com/news/TTN_392528.html |date=2 February 2022 |website=www.tradearabia.com}}</ref>

The island was previously used for military training.<ref name="hotspot"/> In May 2018 the government contracted Kuredu Holdings (the developer of [[Kurendhoo|Kuredu]] island) to develop an airport.<ref name="hotspot"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Government contracts Kuredu Holdings to develop Madivaru Airport |url=https://psmnews.mv/en/34799 |access-date=3 February 2022 |work=PSMnews.mv |language=en}}</ref> The project required reclaiming three hectares of land.<ref name="hotspot">{{cite news |title=Maldives to develop airport in Lhaviyani tourist hotspot |url=https://maldives.net.mv/25914/maldives-to-develop-airport-in-lhaviyani-tourist-hotspot/ |access-date=3 February 2022 |work=Maldives Insider |date=30 May 2018}}</ref>
The construction contractor was [[Beijing Urban Construction Group]] (BUCG).<ref>{{cite news |title=Chinese firm to build airport in Maldives' Lhaviyani tourist hotspot |url=https://maldives.net.mv/27373/chinese-firm-to-build-airport-in-maldives-lhaviyani-tourist-hotspot/ |access-date=3 February 2022 |work=Maldives Insider |date=24 September 2018}}</ref>

It is operated by Island Aviation Services Limited (IASL).<ref>{{cite news |title=Madivaru Airport handed over to IASL |url=https://psmnews.mv/en/97598 |access-date=3 February 2022 |work=PSMnews.mv |language=en}}</ref>

